     "id": "b3b7d62cea9cab706db6d93e1524c40fe7e59c81",
     "tree_id": "1cd7a0e3de2836e8239667c33b9c855aa11e8787",
     "message": "ifa: partition RUNS_ON by the evidence_source its writer stamps\n\ncodex found that repo_dependency's RUNS_ON constraint cannot work. Workload\nmaterialization writes the identical (WorkloadInstance)-[:RUNS_ON]->(Platform)\nshape from workload_materializer.go, and it is live \u2014 wired through\ncmd/reducer. Type and endpoint labels are the same on both writers, so on any\ngraph where workload materialization has run, its edges appear as spurious\nextras in this family's exact set and the family can never pass.\n\nThe earlier derivation searched for competing writers inside the package that\nowns the family's templates, found only a dead builder, and concluded RUNS_ON\nwas single-writer. The live competitor lives in another package with its own\nCypher constants, invisible to that search.\n\nProvenance is the right partition because production already draws the line\nthere: retractRepoRunsOnEdgesCypher deletes WHERE rel.evidence_source =\n$evidence_source, so this family owns the RUNS_ON edges it stamped, not the\nRUNS_ON population. The gate now asserts what the retract reaps.\n\nRemoving RUNS_ON instead would have been the silent direction. The resolver's\nedges are live production output, and this exact template has a recorded\nzero-row no-op on NornicDB v1.1.11 \u2014 the class the absolute expected set\nexists to catch. Dropping it would let that regression pass forever.\n\nThe constraint references reducer.CrossRepoEvidenceSource rather than a copied\nliteral, so it cannot drift from what the writer stamps; that wiring is pinned\nby its own test. Disabling the filter makes the partition test red with the\nother writer's edge leaking in.",
     "timestamp": "2026-08-10T06:54:35Z",
